ANGL====================================================================

    Comment
    -------

    NDT ENTI1 : Number of time steps 
    RXT TAB1  : problem description to compute the closed containment
                content evolution with time


    Description :
    -----------

  The ENCEINTE procedure computes, from an initial state, the evolution in
 time of a gazeous mixture in a closed containment.


CHAP{Overview}

  The initial state can either be a uniform state or the result of a previous
 computation.

  The gazeous mixture is basically composed of air but it can contain one or
 several gases like: steam, H2, N2, He, O2, CO and CO2. Noncondensable gases
 and steam are perfect gases (version V0), even steam can be a real gas
 (version V1, work in progress).

  In presence of steam wall condensation can occur if locally conditions are
 satisfied (Pvap > Psat). The wall condensation model is a Chilton-Colburn
 type using a natural convection correlation along a vertical plane plate.

  We make out 4 types of boundary conditions: the injection zones
 (leak), outlets and valves, thermal walls and passive walls:

  - the injection zones are surfaces of prescribed values. It concerns the
 velocity, the mixture temperature, air and all other constitutive gases if
 they are present: steam, H2, N2, He, O2, CO and CO2. It is necessary to
 specify the mass flow rate for the mixture (kg/s), the inlet temperature (oC)
 the mass flow rate for air and each present gases function of time (see table
 'scenario' of Breches).

  - at the outlets, the mass flow rate is imposed (forced ventilation) or data
 at the exit are prescribed to compute there the pressure loss (valve) (see
 table 'scenario' of 'Sorties'). 

  - at thermal walls, the velocity is set to zero. The temperature is either
 prescribed in time (entry TIMP), set at an initial value which can be modified
 after by personnal procedure (entry ECHANP) or the result of a thermics
 computation (entry THERMP). It is possible to couple thermal equations for
 walls and the flow in an implicit way (entry THERCO). In presence of steam
 these walls are able to condense steam. They are impermeable for all
 noncondensable gases.

  - at passive walls, the velocity is set to zero, and the walls are
 adiabatic/impermeable for all other unknowns (temperature steam and
 noncondensable gases). They are obtained by difference between the meshes of
 the boundary of the fluid domain, the thermal walls, the inlets and the
 outlets.

  Hence, boundary conditions are well posed according to the transient problem
 to solve.

  The turbulent motion of the mixture is modeled either with a constant eddy
 viscosity model, a length scale model or a K-epsilon model (entry MODTURB).
 Absence of entry MODTURB means the flow is laminar.

  A Spray model is avaliable.

  A Mass condensation model is in progress.

  The preparation of a computation consists to fill entries of the RXT table
 and then call the ENCEINTE procedure with this table. Therein, the RXT table
 is achieved with three new tables:
  - the table rxt.'GEO' contains all the objects concerning the geometry.
  - the table rxt.'TBT' is a work table and contains all the objects needed
 for the computation except the unknowns and the geometry. 
  - the table rxt.'TIC' contains all the unknowns at the last known time as
 well as data depending on time evolution.

  Hence, the RXT entries given by the user are not modified.

  Entries and their meaning are following :


CHAP{Geometrical objects}

 rxt.'vtf'    = GEO1   ; fluid mesh (mandatory)
 rxt.'pi'     = POI1   ; point inside the fluid domain which will be used
                         to prescribe the pressure (mandatory).
 rxt.'axe'    = GEO2   ; axe if 2D AXI

  As many inlets (Breches in french) as mandatory can be defined thanks to the
 name of each inlet, it flow direction and mesh:

 rxt . 'Breches' = 'TABLE'                     ; Breches' data
 rxt . 'Breches' . MOT1              = 'TABLE' ; data for the inlet MOT1
 rxt . 'Breches' . MOT1 . 'Maillage' = GEO1    ; mesh of MOT1
 rxt . 'Breches' . MOT1 . 'diru'     = POI1    ; flow direction

 with MOT1, the name of the inlet to be chosen by the user.

  As many outlets (Sorties in french) as mandatory can be defined thanks to 
 the name of each outlet, it flow direction and mesh:

 rxt . 'Sorties' = 'TABLE'                     ; Sorties' data
 rxt . 'Sorties' . MOT1              = 'TABLE' ; data for the outlet MOT1
 rxt . 'Sorties' . MOT1 . 'Maillage' = GEO1    ; mesh of MOT1
 rxt . 'Sorties' . MOT1 . 'diru'     = POI1    ; flow direction

 with MOT1, the name of the outlet to be chosen by the user.

  As many surface at a prescribed temperature (TIMP) as mandatory can be
 defined thanks to the name of each surface and it mesh:

 rxt . 'TIMP' = 'TABLE'                      ; TIMP data
 rxt . 'TIMP' . MOT1              = 'TABLE'  ; data for the surface MOT1
 rxt . 'TIMP' . MOT1 . 'MAILLAGE' = GEO1     ; mesh of MOT1

 with MOT1, the name of the surface at a prescribed temperature to be chosen
 by the user.
 
  Exemple. We define two inlets A and B by:

 rxt . 'Breches' = 'TABL' ;
 rxt . 'Breches' . 'A'              = 'TABL'      ;
 rxt . 'Breches' . 'A' . 'Maillage' = surfacea    ;
 rxt . 'Breches' . 'A' . 'diru'     = (0. 0. 1.)  ;
 rxt . 'Breches' . 'B'              = 'TABL'      ;
 rxt . 'Breches' . 'B' . 'Maillage' = surfaceb    ;
 rxt . 'Breches' . 'B' . 'diru'     = (0. -1. 0.) ;


CHAP{Physical models}

PART{Steam state law}

 rxt . 'VERSION' = (MOT1) ;
 with MOT1=('V0') if perfect gas (default value), 'V1' if real gas (in test) 


PART{Wall thermics}

  A. Activation
  -------------

  Some walls are meshed in volume and not only in surface to compute therein
 temperature evolution in time due to heat exchange with fluid and ambient.
 Global information are the following:

 rxt . 'THERMP' = (LOG1) ; VRAI if wall thermics (FAUX by default).
 rxt . 'THERCO' = (LOG2) ; VRAI if implicit coupling between wall and fluid
 rxt . 'ECHAN'  = FLOT1  ; heat exchange coef. fluid / wall (W/m2/oC)

 In case of steam condensation at walls, FLOT1 is a threshold value. The
 effective convective heat transfer coefficient HT is computed according to the
 HTC value given by the correlation associated with the condensation model, and
 HT = max(FLOT1,HTC).


  B. Wall properties
  ------------------

  For each wall MOT1, wall properties are given by:

 rxt . 'PAROIS' . MOT1 . 'vtp'    = GEO1  ; mesh of MOT1
 rxt . 'PAROIS' . MOT1 . 'LAMBDA' = FLOT1 ; thermal conductivity (W/m/oC)
 rxt . 'PAROIS' . MOT1 . 'ROCP'   = FLOT2 ; rho*Cp (J/m3/oC)
 rxt . 'PAROIS' . MOT1 . 'Tp0'    = FLOT3 ; initial temperature (oC)

 For instance, we define 'Peinture' and 'Acier' walls by:  

 rxt . 'PAROIS' = 'TABL' ;
 rxt . 'PAROIS' . 'Peinture' = 'TABL'              ;
 rxt . 'PAROIS' . 'Peinture' .'vtp'    = mpeinture ;
 rxt . 'PAROIS' . 'Peinture' .'LAMBDA' = 0.2       ;
 rxt . 'PAROIS' . 'Peinture' .'ROCP'   = 1.E6      ;
 rxt . 'PAROIS' . 'Peinture' .'Tp0'    = 60.       ;
 rxt . 'PAROIS' . 'Acier' = 'TABL'            ;
 rxt . 'PAROIS' . 'Acier' . 'vtp'    = macier ;
 rxt . 'PAROIS' . 'Acier' . 'LAMBDA' = 15.    ;
 rxt . 'PAROIS' . 'Acier' . 'ROCP'   = 3.9E6  ;
 rxt . 'PAROIS' . 'Acier' . 'Tp0'    = 60.    ;


PART{Prescribed wall temperatures}

  A. Internal walls
  -----------------

  At the fluid domain boundaries, heat exchange can occur with a prescribed
 wall temperature via a heat exchange coefficient. It allows to modelize a
 condensor. Three zones are possible.

 The value of the heat exchange coefficient is the default value if there is
 no condensation o is the lowest value if condensation.

 rxt . 'TIMP'                     = 'TABL' ; data for prescribed wall temp.
 rxt . 'TIMP' . MOT1              = 'TABL' ; data at the internal wall MOT1
 rxt . 'TIMP' . MOT1 . 'MAILLAGE' = GEO1   ; mesh of the surface MOT1
 rxt . 'TIMP' . MOT1 . 'ECHAN'    = FLOT1  ; heat exchange coef. (W/m2/oC)
 rxt . 'TIMP' . MOT1 . 't'        = LREE1  ; time (s)
 rxt . 'TIMP' . MOT1 . 'TIMP'     = LREE2  ; prescribed temperature (oC)

 with MOT1 the name of the internal wall to be chosen by the user.
 In particular, it modelizes wall condensor. 

 In case of steam condensation, FLOT1 is a threshold value. If we notice HTC,
 the heat exchange coefficient computed by using the correlation according to
 the condensation model, the effective heat exchange coefficient HT is computed
 by: HT = max(FLOT1,HTC).

  Another way to impose constant prescribed inner wall temperature is to use
 ECHANP. Only one wall can be defined. This structure will be suppressed in the
 future. Hence, don't use it. Nevertheless :

 rxt . 'ECHANP' = 'TABL' ; 
 rxt . 'ECHANP' . 'MAILLAGE' = GEO1 ; 
 rxt . 'ECHANP' . 'ECHAN'    = FLOT1 ; 
 rxt . 'ECHANP' . 'TMUR'     = FLOT2 ;


  B. External walls
  -----------------

  External walls can exchange heat with the ambient via a heat exchange
 coefficient between the wall and the ambient. For that, the ECHEXT boolean
 have to be activated.

 rxt . 'ECHEXT' = (LOG1) ; VRAI if external walls are defined (FAUX)
 
 rxt . 'parext' = GEO1   ; mesh of the external wall
 rxt . 'HEXT'   = FLOT1  ; heat exchange coefficient (W/m2/oC)
 rxt . 'TPEXT'  = FLOT2  ; external temperature (oC)

 Example. If the enclosure is semi-immersed, two zones can be defined (one for
 the immersed part and the other for the emmerged part) by using the PROCHEXT
 procedure.  The two zones are identified by the water height, which has to be
 provided to the model (HZ).  This procedure is to be called from a personnal
 procedure (see PRCPERSO entry). We refer to the notice of the procedure
 PRCHEXT to adress the way to call it in the PERSO procedure. The entries
 provided may depend on time (look at pressuhext1.dgibi and pressuhext2.dgibi
 as examples).


PART{Initial conditions}

  A. Mixture definition
  ---------------------

 rxt . 'VAPEUR' = (LOG1) ; presence of stem (FAUX)
 rxt . 'H2'     = (LOG2) ; presence of hydrogen (FAUX)
 rxt . 'HE'     = (LOG3) ; presence of helium (FAUX)
 rxt . 'N2'     = (LOG4) ; presence of nitrogen (FAUX)
 rxt . 'O2'     = (LOG5) ; presence of oxygen (FAUX)
 rxt . 'CO'     = (LOG6) ; presence of carbon monoxyde (FAUX)
 rxt . 'CO2'    = (LOG7) ; presence de carbon dioxyde (FAUX)


  B. Mixture composition
  ----------------------

  The initial mixture composition is uniform over the fluid domain.

 rxt . 'TF0' = FLOT1 ; initial temperature (oC)
 rxt . 'PT0' = FLOT2 ; initial pressure (Pa)
 rxt . MOT1  = FLOT3 ; mass fraction of a component if present
 with MOT1='Yvap0','Yhe0','Yh20','Yo20','Yn20','Yco0','Yco20'.

 Remark : It is not necessary to define the air component as the sum of mass
 fractions is 1.


  C. Proprietes du melange
  ------------------------

* Physical properties of the gazeous mixture
* Gas constant (J/kg/K)
 Rgh2  = 4156.5
 Rghe  = 2078.25
 Rgo2  = 259.8
 Rgn2  = 296.9
 Rgco2 = 188.9
 Rgco  = 296.9
 Rgvap = 461.513
 Rgair = 287.1

 muair =  1.800e-5 : dynamic viscosity (air)  (kg/m/s)
 alf   =  1.800e-5 : thermal diffusivity      (m2/s)
 lamair=  2.580e-2 : thermal conductivity     (W/m/oC)
 db    =  1.000e-5 : Brownian diffusivity     (m2/s)
 Cpvap =  1700.0   : steam specific heat capacity 
                     at constant pressure     (J/oC/kg)
 Lv    =  2.3E6    : latent heat              (J/kg)

 The heat capacity for non condensable gases are computed with temperature
 evolution by the CALCP procedure.


PART{Boundary conditions}

  Boundaries with walls are treated in B/ and C/. Hereafter we deal with fluid
 inlets and outlets.


  A. Fluid inlets
  ---------------

  For each fluid inlet MOT1, the mass flow rate and the inlet temperature
 evolutions are given in the scenario table in the following way: 

 rxt.'Breches'.MOT1.'scenario'        = 'TABL' ; inlet MOT1 scenario
 rxt.'Breches'.MOT1.'scenario'.'t'    = LREE1  : time evolution (s) 
 rxt.'Breches'.MOT1.'scenario'.'qair' = LREE2  : air mass flow rate (kg/s)
 rxt.'Breches'.MOT1.'scenario'. MOT2  = LREE3  : MOT2 mass flow rate (kg/s)
 rxt.'Breches'.MOT1.'scenario'.'tinj' = LREE4  : inlet temperature (oC)

 with MOT2='qeau','qhe','qh2','qo2','qn2','qco' ou 'qco2' depending of the
 mixture composition. As air is always present, 'qair' entry is always
 specified.

 For example, a steam mass flow rate of 50 g/s at 150oC is specified at the
 inlet labeled A by:

 rxt.'Breches'.'A'.'scenario'        = 'TABL' ;
 rxt.'Breches'.'A'.'scenario'.'t'    = 'PROG' 0.0    1000.0 ;
 rxt.'Breches'.'A'.'scenario'.'qair' = 'PROG' 0.000  0.000  ;
 rxt.'Breches'.'A'.'scenario'.'qeau' = 'PROG' 0.050  0.050  ;
 rxt.'Breches'.'A'.'scenario'.'tinj' = 'PROG' 150.0  150.0  ;

  For steam, gas and liquid flow separation is done at boiling
 temperature. Hence, steam quality xsi is defined by:
 xsi = (h_{vap,inj}-h_{liq,ebu}) / (h_{vap,sat}-h_{liq,ebu}), 
 with h_{vap,inj} = h(P_{vap,inj},T_{inj}), 
      h_{vap,ebu} = hvap(P,Tsat(P)), 
      h_{liq,ebu} = hliq(P,Tsat(P)), 
      P, the (total) containment pressure.

  The liquid part goes directly inside the sump. Initial sump mass
 entry is rxt . 'Mliq0' (in kg, 0. by default).


  B. Fluid outlets
  ----------------

  For each MOT1 outlet, two models are available: the former is a forced
 ventilation ('Qext' entry), the latter is a valve ('Pext' entry).  In the
 first case, the forced mass flow rate evolution is given ; in the second case,
 the mass flow rate is proportional to the pressure loss according to: 
    P - Pext = Kext (U ** Bext).  
 Hence, ambient pressure as well as valve parameters have to be given. One's
 has to note that sonic conditions are applied if necessary at the valve
 outlet.

 rxt . 'Sorties' . MOT1 . 'scenario'             = 'TABL' ; MOT1 scenario
 rxt . 'Sorties' . MOT1 . 'scenario' . 't'       = LREE1  ; time evolution (s)
 rxt . 'Sorties' . MOT1 . 'scenario' . 'Section' = FLOT1  ; outlet section (m2)

  Cas a : 'Qext' : forced ventilation

 rxt . 'Sorties' . MOT1 . 'scenario' . 'Qext'    = LREE2 ; mass flow rate (kg/s)

  Cas b : 'Pext' : valve

 rxt . 'Sorties' . MOT1 . 'scenario' . 'Pext'    = LREE2 ; ambient pressure (Pa)
 rxt . 'Sorties' . MOT1 . 'scenario' . 'Kext'    = LREE3 ; head loss coef.
 rxt . 'Sorties' . MOT1 . 'scenario' . 'Bext'    = LREE4 ; head loss exponent

 Remark : To suppress the outlet MOT1 for the transient, FLOT1 at the Section
 entry can be set to zero (<1.e-10) with a PERSO procedure.
 
  Example. Hereafter we define the outlet S :

 rxt . 'Sorties' = 'TABL' ;

 rxt . 'Sorties' . 'S' = 'TABL' ;
 rxt . 'Sorties' . 'S' . 'Maillage' = ms         ;
 rxt . 'Sorties' . 'S' . 'diru'     = (0. 0. 1.) ;
 rxt . 'Sorties' . 'S' . 'scenario' = 'TABL'     ;
 rxt . 'Sorties' . 'S' . 'scenario' . 'Section' = 4.68e-4           ;
 rxt . 'Sorties' . 'S' . 'scenario' . 't'       = 'PROG' 0.0 1000.0 ;

  Case a : 'Qext'
 rxt . 'Sorties' . 'S' . 'scenario' . 'Qext'    = 'PROG' 1.     1.    ;

  Case b : 'Pext'
 rxt . 'Sorties' . 'S' . 'scenario' . 'Pext'    = 'PROG' 1.e5   1.e5  ;
 rxt . 'Sorties' . 'S' . 'scenario' . 'Kext'    = 'PROG' 1.000  1.000 ;
 rxt . 'Sorties' . 'S' . 'scenario' . 'Bext'    = 'PROG' 0.75   0.75  ;


  C. Low mass fluxes
  ------------------

  When the mass flow rate is very low, it can be modelise as a diffusive mass
 flux coming from a surface MOT1:

 rxt . 'XFIMP' = 'TABL'                    ; diffusive fluxes data
 rxt . 'XFIMP' . MOT1 = 'TABL'             ; MOT1 surface data
 rxt . 'XFIMP' . MOT1 . 'MAILLAGE' = GEO1  ; mesh
 rxt . 'XFIMP' . MOT1 . 't'        = LREE1 ; time evolution (s)
 rxt . 'XFIMP' . MOT1 . MOT2       = LREE2 ; mass flow rate (kg/s)

 with MOT1 the name of the surface chosen by the user
      MOT2='qeau','qair','qhe','qh2','qo2','qn2','qco','qco2'


PART{Turbulence}

  If this entry is ommited the flow is considered laminar.

 < rxt . 'MODTURB' =  MOT1 ; > type of turbulence model
 with MOT1='NUTURB','LMEL' or 'KEPSILON'.

 a/  rxt . 'MODTURB'  =  'NUTURB'  ; constant eddy viscosity
     rxt . 'NUT'      =  FLOT1     ; Nut value (m2/s) 

 b/  rxt . 'MODTURB'  =  'LMEL'    ; length scale model
     rxt . 'LMEL'     =  FLOT1     ; lm value (m)

 c/  rxt . 'MODTURB'  = 'KEPSILON' ; k-epsilon model
     < rxt . 'FPAROI' = LOGIQUE    ; > wall function (FAUX) 
     < rxt . 'YP'     = FLOT1      ; > reference y+ value 

 KEPSILON option is only available with the EFM1 algorithm 
 (see ALGO entry).


PART{Spray}

 rxt.'ASPER'  = (LOG1) ; VRAI if spray model is activated.

 Spray model needs  rxt.'VAPEUR' = VRAI ;

 rxt.'aspinj' = GEO1 ; Mesh of the injection surface 
                       for the dispersed phase.
 rxt.'toitf'  = GEO2 ; Mesh of the upper surface 
                       of the fluid volume
 rxt.'rod'    = GEO3 ; Density of the dispersed phase.
 rxt.'Cpd'    = GEO4 ; Specific heat of the dispersed phase.
 rxt.'scenario'.'vzinj' = LIST1 ; List of injection velocities
                                  (vertical) for the dispersed phase.
 rxt.'scenario'.'xdinj' = LIST2 ; List of volumic fraction of the
                                  dispersed phase at injection.
 rxt.'scenario'.'tdinj' = LIST3 ; List of dispersed phase temperature
                                  at injection
 rxt.'scenario'.'ddinj' = LIST4 ; List of droplet diameters at injection


PART{Bulk condensation (in progress)}

 rxt.'CONDMAS' = (LOG1) ; VRAI when bulk condensation model is activated (FAUX)


PART{PAR (Passive Autocatalytic Recombiner)}

  Recombiners can be defined only if these constituants are present :
 H2, N2, O2, VAPEUR

  As many PARs as necessary are defined by the following sequence (rank=1,n):

 rxt . 'RECOMB' = 'TABLE'                     ; data for PARs
 rxt . 'RECOMB' . rank = 'TABL'               ; Par number rank data
 rxt . 'RECOMB' . rank . 'PAREXT'   = MAIL1   ; PAR walls
 rxt . 'RECOMB' . rank . 'ENTREE'   = MAIL2   ; PAR inlet mesh
 rxt . 'RECOMB' . rank . 'SORTIE'   = MAIL3   ; PAR outlet mesh
 rxt . 'RECOMB' . rank . 'direntr'  = POI1    ; inlet direction
 rxt . 'RECOMB' . rank . 'dirsort'  = POI2    ; outlet direction
 rxt . 'RECOMB' . rank . 'NSECTION' = (ENTI1) ; PAR numbers at that rank
 rxt . 'RECOMB' . rank . 'A'        = (FLOT1) ; h2 consumption law coef.
 rxt . 'RECOMB' . rank . 'B'        = (FLOT2) ; h2 consumption law coef.
 rxt . 'RECOMB' . rank . 'XH2ON'    = (FLOT3) ; switch on condition
 rxt . 'RECOMB' . rank . 'XH2OFF'   = (FLOT4) ; switch off condition
 rxt . 'RECOMB' . rank . 'BETA'     = (FLOT5) ; h2 consumption law coef.

  By default, the number of unit (NSECTION entry) is 1.

  The PAR hydrogen consumption law is the one given by SIEMENS. By default, 
 they correspond to the SIEMENS FR90/1-150 model:
  - A and B : Coefficients of the linear function in pressure
    (0.48D-8 kg/s/Pa et 0.58D-3 kg/s by default)
  - XH2ON  : Swith on molar fraction of H2 (0.005)
  - XH2OFF : Switch off molar fraction of H2 (0.005)
  - BETA   : Correction for low H2 concentration (0.2)


PART{Wall condensation}

 rxt . 'MODCOND' = (MOT1) ; wall condensation model
 with MOT1=('CHIL0') or 'CHIL1'.

 a/ rxt . 'MODCOND' = 'CHIL0' : Jv = Jstand = kc rho (Yv - Yvsat)
 Chilton-Colburn model type. It is only for very small steam mass fraction.
 We use a natural convection correlation for sensible heat transfer.

 b/ rxt . 'MODCOND' = 'CHIL1' : Jv = Jstand / (1-Yvsat)
 This model comes from a volumic mass Fick's law with. The steam diffusivity
 coefficient in the mixture is computed from RALOC formula.


CHAP{Numerical parameters}

PART{RXT table}

  It is possible to define a personal procedure to be called at the begining of
 each time step.

 rxt . 'PERSO'    = (LOG1)  ; PERSO procedure activation (FAUX)
 rxt . 'PRCPERSO' = MOT1    ; name of the procedure
 rxt . 'TABPERSO' = TAB1    ; associated data

 rxt . 'DT0'      =  FLOT1  ; time step value (s)
 rxt . 'IMPR'     = (ENTI1) ; (0) ou 1 if further listing informations
 rxt . 'epsi'     = (FLOT1) ; smallest distance between mesh points (1.E-4)

 rxt . 'FRPREC'   = (ENTI1) ; frequency for matrix preconditionning (1)
 rxt . 'FCPRECT'  = (ENTI2) ; frequency for update matrix prec. based 
                              on time step loop (1)
 rxt . 'FCPRECI'  = (ENTI3) ; frequency for update of matrix prec. based 
                              on inner iteration loop (1)

 Warning: if FPREC is prescribed, default values for FCPRECT and FCPRECI are 5
 instead of 1.

 rxt . 'ALGO'     = (MOT1)  ; Algorithm choice ('IMPL')
 with MOT1='IMPL' if semi implicit and 'EFM1' if semi explicit.

 rxt . 'DISCR'    = (MOT1)  ; spatial discretization for velocity and 
                              scalar fields (except pressure) ('MACRO').
 rxt . 'KPRE'     = (MOT2)  ; spatial discretization for pressure ('CENTRE')

 Warning: all couples MOT1/MOT2 doesn't make sense (see NAVI and MODEL notices)

 rxt . 'STAB'     = (FLOT1) ; stability coef. for MACRO elements (1.).

 rxt . 'MAXELPRE' = (ENTI1) ; Maximum number of elements for which a direct
                              method (Cholesky) is used to invert the pressure
                              matrix (150000).
 rxt . 'TYPINV'   = (ENTI2) ; iterative solver choice (voir KRES) (3)
 rxt . 'RESIDU'   = (FLOT1) ; residual for iterative solver (1.E-20).
 rxt . 'NTIT'     = (ENTI3) ; Number of iterations for iterative solver (800)

 rxt . 'GRAPH'    = (LOG1)  ; display of mean value (FAUX)
 rxt . 'DETMAT'   = (LOG1)  ; matrix are killed at the end of the computation
                              to limit file output size (VRAI)
 rxt . 'REINIT'   = (LOG1)  ; Re-built the scenario (FAUX)
 rxt . 'CORTEMP'  = (LOG1)  ; energy balance control (VRAI)


PART{Model restrictions}

 No real gas modeling for steam
 No implicit k-epsilon tubulence model
 No bulk condensation model
 No symetry plane for boundary conditions 
 Numerical scheme devoted to large time steps
 No parallelism capability


PART{Guidelines}

  A. Change in the type of boundary conditions
  --------------------------------------------

  Lorsque par exemple une breche cesse d'emettre, la nature des conditions aux
 limites change a la breche. Pour prendre en compte cela, le plus simple est
 de sauvegarder le premier calcul, de decrire le 'nouveau' probleme sans la
 breche et de surcharger la table des inconnues par celle de l'ancien calcul.

  1/ One gets the rxt.'TIC' table from the previous simulation
     'OPTI' 'REST' 'MONFIC.sauv' ; 'REST' ;
     tic = rxt . 'TIC' ;
  2/ Also (eventualy) the mesh
     opti rest 'MONMAIL.sauv';
     rest ;
  3/ One describe (fully) the new problem
     rxt = 'TABL' ;
     rxt . 'vtf' =  my mesh ;
     rxt . ... etc
  4/ One initialize the new TIC entry with the previous one
     rxt . 'TIC' = tic ;


  B. SAUV file size
  -----------------

  It is possible to suppress the matrices (see rxt . 'DETMAT') in order to
 reduce the size of the file output ('SAUV' file).


CHAP{Computational results}

PART{rxt.'TIC' table}

  The TIC table contains in particular: 
  - list in time (LT) 0D (mean values) to plot scaler quantity evolution in
 time according to the time list rxt.'TIC'.'LTPS' de TIC.
  - multi dimensional fields (CHPO) at the last time computed.  

 For each index, we indicate the index type, his name and the index contente.


  A. 0D fields
  ------------

 Index          Object
 Type & Value   Type       Comments

 MOT    Tps      FLOTTANT : Physical time
 MOT    NUPADT   ENTIER   : time step nummer.
 MOT    LTPS     LISTREEL : list of computed times.
 MOT    DT       FLOTTANT : time step

 MOT    LMAXU    LISTREEL : List in time (LT) of maximum velocity  (m/s)
 MOT    Rhomn    LISTREEL : LT mean density of the mixture (kg/m3)
 MOT    Rhomv    LISTREEL : LT mean density of steam (kg/m3)
 MOT    Rhom?    LISTREEL : LT mean density for ? (kg/m3)
 with '?'='air','he','h2','o2','n2','co','co2'

 MOT    PT       LISTREEL : LT thermodynamic pressure (Pa)
 MOT    Tfm      LISTREEL : LT mean temperature of the mixture (oC)
 MOT    Remn     LISTREEL : LT specific energy of the mixture (J/m3)

 MOT    Econv    LISTREEL : LT energy extracted by convection (J/m3)
 MOT    Econd    LISTREEL : LT energy extracted by condensation (J/m3)
 MOT    Hcond    LISTREEL : LT enthalpy extracted by condensation(J/m3)

 MOT    Easpe    LISTREEL : LT energy extracted by spray (J/m3)
 MOT    Haspe    LISTREEL : LT enthalpy extracted by spray (J/m3)
 MOT    Qaspe    LISTREEL : LT spray flow rate (kg/s)

 MOT    Rgpm     LISTREEL : LT perfect gaz constant for the mixture (J/kg/K)
 MOT    Cvm      LISTREEL : LT heat capacity at constant volume (J/kg/K)
 MOT    Cpm      LISTREEL : LT heat capacity at constant pressure (J/kg/K)
 MOT    Gamm     LISTREEL : LT Cpm.Cvm ratio

 MOT    Minj     LISTREEL : LT injected mass (kg)
 MOT    Mrest    LISTREEL : LT resident mass (kg)

 MOT    Mcond    LISTREEL : LT condensed mass (kg)
 MOT    Mcondw   LISTREEL : LT condensed mass (THERMP) (kg)
 MOT    Mcond0   LISTREEL : LT condensed mass (ECHANP) (kg)
 MOT    Mcond1i  LISTREEL : LT condensed mass (TTIMPi) (kg)

 MOT    Qc       LISTREEL : LT Total flow rate for condensation (kg/s)
 MOT    Qcw      LISTREEL : LT condensation flow rate for (THERMP) (kg/s)
 MOT    Qc1i     LISTREEL : LT condensation flow rate for (TTIMPi) (kg/s)
 MOT    Qc0      LISTREEL : LT condensation flow rate for (ECHANP) (kg/s)

 MOT    mdTf     LISTREEL : LT local TF correction (min) (oC)
 MOT    MdTf     LISTREEL : LT local TF correction (max) (oC)
 MOT    Mdr?     LISTREEL : LT mass correction for ? (kg)
 with '?'='vap','air','he','h2','o2','n2','co','co2'


 *--- Prescribed wall temperatures (TIMP) ---*

 MOT    TIMP     TABLE : data for TIMP

 TIMP . nomi     TABLE, data for the wall nomi
 MOT    Ltbpi    LISTREEL : LT, wall temperature (oC)


 *--- Inlets (Breches) ---*

 MOT    Breches   TABLE : inlets data

 Breches . nomi  TABLE, data for the inlet nomi
 MOT    guj      LISTREEL : LT, inlet velocity (m/s)
 MOT    Qj       LISTREEL : LT, inlet mass flow rate (kg/s)
 MOT    Tinj     LISTREEL : LT, inlet temperature (kg/s)
 MOT    Hj       LISTREEL : LT, inlet enthalpy (J/kg)
 MOT    Ej       LISTREEL : LT, inlet energy (J/kg)
 MOT    Ksi      LISTREEL : LT, inlet steam quality (kg/s)
 MOT    Qlj      LISTREEL : LT, inlet liquid mass flow rate (kg/s)
 MOT    Hlj      LISTREEL : LT, inlet liquid enthalpy (J/kg)

 Use. rxt . 'Breches' . nomi . 'Tinj' contains the LT of the 
 inlet temperature at the nomi inlet. Associated times are those 
 of rxt . 'TIC' . 'LTPS'

 *--- RECOMBINER ---*

 MOT    RECOMB   TABLE    : PARs information

 RECOMB . i      TABLE, data of the PAR number rank
 MOT    DEB      LISTREEL : LT, mass flow rate (kg/s)
 MOT    Uin      LISTREEL : LT, inlet velocity (m/s)
 MOT    Uout     LISTREEL : LT, outlet temperature (oC)
 MOT    Tin      LISTREEL : LT, inlet temperature (oC)
 MOT    Tout     LISTREEL : LT, outlet temperature (oC)
 MOT    Tpla     LISTREEL : LT, catalytic plates temperature (oC)
 MOT    EFF      LISTREEL : LT, PPAR efficiency
 MOT    Hin      LISTREEL : LT, inlet enthalpy (J/kg)
 MOT    Hout     LISTREEL : LT, outlet enthalpy (J/kg)
 MOT    R?E      LISTREEL : LT, inlet density for ? (kg/m3)
 MOT    R?S      LISTREEL : LT, outlet density for ? (kg/m3)
 MOT    Y?E      LISTREEL : LT, inlet mass fraction for ?
 MOT    Y?S      LISTREEL : LT, outlet mass fraction for ?
 avec '?'='H2O','HE','H2','O2','N2','CO','CO2'.

 Use. rxt . 'RECOMB' . 2 . 'DEB' is the mass flow rate LT through the
 second PAR according to the list of times given at rxt . 'TIC' . 'LTPS'


  B. CFD Fields
  -------------

 Index           Object
 Type & Value    Type       Comments


 MOT    UN       CHPOINT  : current velocity (m/s)
 MOT    UNM      CHPOINT  : previous velocity (m/s)
 MOT    PRES     CHPOINT  : pressure (Pa)
 MOT    TF       CHPOINT  : current fluid temperature (oC)
 MOT    TFNM     CHPOINT  : previous fluid temperature field (oC)
 MOT    TP       CHPOINT  : current wall temperature (oC)
 MOT    TPNM     CHPOINT  : previous wall temperature (oC)
 MOT    RHO      CHPOINT  : current density (kg/m3)
 MOT    RHONM    CHPOINT  : previous density (kg/m3)
 MOT    RVP      CHPOINT  : current steam density (kg/m3)
 MOT    YVAP     CHPOINT  : current steam mass fraction

 MOT    R?       CHPOINT  : current ? density (kg/m3)
 MOT    Y?       CHPOINT  : current ? mass fraction
 avec ?='AIR','HE','H2','O2','N2','CO','CO2'

 MOT    Mu       CHPOINT  : dynamic viscosity (kg/m/s)
 MOT    NU       CHPOINT  : cinematic viscosity (m2/s)
 MOT    NUEFF    CHPOINT  : effective cinematic viscosity (m2/s)

 MOT    KHW      CHPOINT  : heat exchange coefficient (THERMP)
 MOT    KH0      CHPOINT  : heat exchange coefficient (ECHANP)
 MOT    KH1i     CHPOINT  : heat exchange coefficient (TIMP)


 *--- Spray ---*

 MOT    VN       CHPOINT  : disperse phase velocity (m/s)
 MOT    TD       CHPOINT  : disperse phase temperature (oC)
 MOT    XD       CHPOINT  : disperse phase molar fraction
 MOT    DD       CHPOINT  : droplets diameter (m)


PART{rxt.'GEO' table}

  GEO table contains MMODEL NAVIER-STOKES objects for the chosen models as well
 as associated meshes (at the same index without $).

 Index           Object
 Type & Value    Type       Comments

 MOT    epsi     FLOTTANT : smallest distance between mesh points
 MOT    $vtf     MMODEL   : fluid model
 MOT    $menvf   MMODEL   : fluid boundary model
 MOT    $axe     MMODEL   : axis or symetry plane
 MOT    $vtp     MMODEL   : thermal walls
 MOT    $mtp1i   MMODEL   : prescribed walls temperature (TIMP)
 MOT    $mtp0    MMODEL   : constant wall temperature (ECHANP)
 MOT    Pimp     MAILLAGE : POI1 where the pressure is fixed
 
